Z||000000||Philphine||01-21-2001||01:28 PM||||<BR>i see them advertised every once in a while and it was offered as an option with my air/fuel meter (for cars that dont have one standard). would there be any advantage to switching to one next time it's needed? can it be used without negatively affecting the ecm? part of what i'm wonderng is if it would help my a/f meter give more stable readings.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe (cheap thrill)<BR>'84 chop top (parts car or project. time will tell)||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||01-21-2001||02:33 PM||||A three wire o2 sensor has a heating element in them that gets them to operating temperature more quickly than a one wire o2 sensor can. An o2 sensor needs to get to about 600 degrees F to start to work. Newer cars use them to help them get lower emissions and better fuel economy because they get the car out of open loop more quickly. The computer does not go into open loop until the o2 sensor reaches operating temperature. There is no difference other than that between the two.<BR>The three wire o2 sensor also allows you to place the o2 sensor further away from the engine, which you cannot do with a single wire o2 sensor because the exhaust gas cools down allot as it goes further down the exhaust system (remember it must reach 600 degrees). <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-21-2001||02:39 PM||||There is no benefit to installing a heated sensor on a stock fiero. Only 4 cylinder motors with the hooker header need one and then only sometimes. With the hooker the sensor is too far down line to stay hot under all conditions. This is generally not an issue with V6 header exhausts. The V6 throws more heat than the 4. Thermal Coated hookers on the 4 should also have less need of the heated O2.<P>It won't hurt the ECM to install one. The heater power is not provided by the ECM. It comes from a switched & fused 12v feed as I remember. been awhile since I worked with one.<P>Your A/F meter will never give "stable" readings. The O2 content of the exhaust is constanly changing. The only real use for an A/F meter is during certain types of engine work where you are trying to check A/F ratio at WOT. Things that change air or fuel flow can make the engine go lean at WOT. Lean burning can blow an engine real quick.<P>At all other times the ECM is constantly adjusting the A/F ratio. The O2 sensor jumps around in responce to this. If you ever do get a stable reading from the O2 sensor durring normal driving something is wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||01-21-2001||03:16 PM||||thanks. no need to spend extra then.||||reg||1
